---
/**
 * What protects a portfolio in a crash. Answer first: fewer stocks. Then the table of
 * what each candidate did in the worst tenth of stock months, and one short section per
 * candidate. The portfolio falls are read from the series; the hit rates and the rest come
 * from the research pages the figure records cite.
 */
import Button from "~/components/Button.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import StatGrid from "~/components/StatGrid.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { formatDollars, formatPct } from "~/lib/charts";
import { loadSeries, seriesFor } from "~/lib/series";

const file = loadSeries("1990");
const long = loadSeries("1929");
const market = seriesFor("market").summary;
const sixtyForty = seriesFor("sixty-forty").summary;
const cautious = seriesFor("cautious").summary;
const longMarket = seriesFor("market", "1929").summary;
const longCautious = seriesFor("cautious", "1929").summary;

const STATS = [
  {
    value: formatPct(market.worstFall.pct),
    unit: formatDollars(market.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough),
    label: "Worst fall, a plain world stock index",
    note: `${file.window.label}, simulated. What $10,000 fell to`,
  },
  {
    value: formatPct(sixtyForty.worstFall.pct),
    unit: formatDollars(sixtyForty.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough),
    label: "Worst fall, 60% stocks and 40% bonds",
    note: `${file.window.label}, simulated`,
  },
  {
    value: formatPct(cautious.worstFall.pct),
    unit: formatDollars(cautious.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough),
    label: "Worst fall, the cautious portfolio",
    note: `${file.window.label}, simulated. About half its money in bonds`,
  },
  {
    value: formatPct(longCautious.worstFall.pct),
    label: "The cautious portfolio, 1929 to 1932",
    note: `Against ${formatPct(longMarket.worstFall.pct)} for all US stocks, ${long.window.label}, simulated`,
  },
];

/**
 * The worst tenth of stock months over each asset's own history: how often the asset rose,
 * and what moving 10% of stocks into it added back in the average such month. Read from
 * the alternatives audit; the windows differ, so the start year is part of each row.
 */
const ROWS = [
  { holding: "Trend following", since: 1985, rose: "69%", added: "+1.08%" },
  { holding: "Long US government bonds", since: 1926, rose: "53%", added: "+0.94%" },
  { holding: "Gold", since: 1975, rose: "56%", added: "+0.92%" },
  { holding: "Cash-like government bills", since: 1926, rose: "—", added: "+0.92%" },
  { holding: "Corporate bonds", since: 1926, rose: "48%", added: "+0.88%" },
  { holding: "Commodities", since: 1926, rose: "36%", added: "+0.74%" },
  { holding: "Bitcoin", since: 2015, rose: "8%", added: "+0.05%" },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="Crash protection"
  description="Compare less stock exposure, bonds, cash and explicit hedges by what they change inside a portfolio, including their costs and failure modes."
  xl
>
  <Hero
    title="What actually protects you in a crash?"
    lede="Start with how much stock-market risk you can afford. Moving money into cash reduces that exposure directly. Bonds and hedges can help in different ways, but each brings a cost and a way to disappoint."
  >
    <p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-ink-2">
      <Verdict word="Settled" gloss="Replacing stocks with cash reduces direct stock exposure." />
      <Verdict word="Too close to call" gloss="Which additional hedge improves the whole portfolio after costs." />
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <StatGrid items={STATS} class="wide mt-10" />

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <p>Over {file.window.label} a plain world stock index took $10,000 down to
    {formatDollars(market.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough)} at its worst; a 60/40 mix, 60% stocks and 40% US
    government bonds, fell to {formatDollars(sixtyForty.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough)}; the
    <a href="/portfolios/cautious/">cautious portfolio</a>, about half in inflation-protected government bonds,
    fell to {formatDollars(cautious.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough)}. These are simulated portfolios built from index
    data, not fund records. The smaller fall has a price: over 96 years, moving 10% of the money from stocks to
    cash cost about 0.77% a year of growth. That is the return forgone in this history, not a guaranteed future
    price of protection. The table below asks how often each holding rose in bad months; it does not establish
    what a small options hedge would add to a complete portfolio.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wide mt-12">
    <h2 class="h2 mb-2" id="the-worst-months">What did what in the worst months</h2>
    <p class="label mb-5">The worst tenth of stock months over each holding's available history: how often it rose, and what moving 10% of stocks into it added back in the average such month.</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" aria-label="What each holding did in the worst tenth of stock months">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-base" style="max-width: 44rem">
        <caption class="sr-only">Each holding, the year its history starts, how often it rose in the worst tenth of stock months, and what moving 10% of stocks into it added back in the average such month</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
            <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-3 font-semibold">Holding</th>
            <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-3 text-right font-semibold">Since</th>
            <th scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-3 text-right font-semibold">Rose in</th>
            <th scope="col" class="label py-2 text-right font-semibold">Added back</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-ink-2">
          {ROWS.map((r) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
              <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-3 text-left font-medium text-ink">{r.holding}</th>
              <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-3 text-right">{r.since}</td>
              <td data-numeric class="py-2 pr-3 text-right">{r.rose}</td>
              <td data-numeric class="py-2 text-right">{r.added}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <p>Only trend following clearly beats cash in the worst months, and it is held on top of stocks in the
    <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">Plus trend portfolio</a>. Long bonds add two hundredths of a percent a
    month over cash; gold matches it; commodities and bitcoin do worse than cash. The windows differ, so read
    the start years.</p>

    <h2 id="long-bonds">Long US government bonds</h2>
    <p>They paid in every growth crash and lost in all three inflation shocks. In the worst stock months since
    1926 they added 0.94% back against 0.92% for cash, and that sliver was bought with swings of 8.4% a year, a
    worst fall of their own of <Figure id="long-treasury-worst-fall" inline showUnit={false} /> against cash,
    and a 40% loss through the late 1970s. Adding 20% of borrowed long bonds on top of the portfolio read +0.34%
    a year over 1929 to 2025 with a range that includes zero, all of it earned inside 1981 to 2020. Since 2020
    stocks and bonds have mostly fallen together. Not added.</p>

    <h2 id="tips">Inflation-protected bonds</h2>
    <p>Inflation-protected and plain US government bonds are one idea: eighteen funds move together at
    <Figure id="tips-nominal-link" inline showUnit={false} /> out of 1. Hold the inflation-protected kind because
    your future spending rises with prices, and take any crash protection from holding fewer stocks. A ten-year
    inflation-protected bond pays <Figure id="tips-real-yield" inline />, the most since 2008. Which fund: SCHP
    and TIP hold the same bonds and SCHP costs a sixth as much; the <a href="/portfolios/cautious/">cautious
    portfolio</a> holds half its money in it and says which account to hold it in.</p>

    <h2 id="tail-funds">Tail-risk funds</h2>
    <p>TAIL holds government bonds and buys put options that can pay when stocks fall. Its bond holdings can
    lose value as rates rise, offsetting some protection from the options. CAOS combines options with changing
    stock exposure, so it is a different construction. Their
    <a href="https://www.cambriafunds.com/assets/docs/Cambria_TAIL_Summary.pdf">TAIL</a> and
    <a href="https://etfarchitect.com/wp-content/uploads/compliance/etf/summary_prospectus/CAOS_Summary_Prospectus.pdf">CAOS prospectuses</a>
    describe those choices.</p>

    <p>Trailing a stock index is not the same as paying that much for insurance. A useful comparison
    puts a small hedge inside the portfolio, identifies the stocks or bonds sold to fund it, and tests a rule
    for using any gains. It should also compare simply holding more cash. The available evidence does not
    establish a preferred hedge or a guarantee of protection.</p>

    <h2 id="buffer-funds">Buffer funds</h2>
    <p>A buffer fund caps your gains for a year in exchange for a cushion on your losses: a typical one charges
    0.79% for a 9% cushion against an 18% cap, and measures itself against the S&amp;P 500 without its
    dividends. Priced on 1,183 twelve-month windows since 1926, the cap-and-cushion package is worth −2.4% to
    −4.1% a year, and the funds' own record of −4.1% a year lands inside that range. You pay for the cushion
    with the gains, every year, whether or not the fall comes.</p>

    <h2 id="cat-bonds">Catastrophe bonds</h2>
    <p>A catastrophe bond is a loan to an insurer that is written off after a named hurricane or earthquake, the
    only holding here whose losses come from weather rather than markets. The funds returned
    <Figure id="cat-bond-fund-return" inline />, about 1% a year over cash, and they can lose 10% in a week, as
    they did after Hurricane Ian in September 2022. The price is the problem: the bonds paid
    <Figure id="cat-bond-spread-multiple" inline /> against 4.9 times in January 2023, the thinnest on record.
    Hold 0% to 3% in a retirement account, or wait; the question reopens at 3.5 times, a number published
    weekly.</p>

    <h2 id="cash">Cash</h2>
    <p>Cash is the surprise. Moving 10% of stocks into cash-like government bills added 0.92% back in the
    average worst stock month since 1926, as much as gold and almost as much as long bonds, for no fee and with
    no fall of its own. A three-month government bill paid 3.88% in August 2026, about 0.2% a year above the past
    year's inflation. In an ordinary brokerage account, BOXX (0.19%) pays a cash-like return as a rise in price,
    so the tax falls when you sell. Cash still cost about 0.77% a year of growth over 96 years: the cheapest
    protection, and not a free one.</p>

    <h2 id="not-protection">Things that look like protection</h2>
    <p>Corporate bonds are government bonds plus a slice of stock risk, moving with government bonds at 0.83 out
    of 1; with the interest-rate risk stripped out the leftover tracks them at only
    <Figure id="credit-spread-link" inline showUnit={false} /> out of 1, but it lost 13% in 2008 while government
    bonds gained 13%. Dividend funds move with the market at 0.84 out of 1 and property funds at 0.82. Bond
    distributions affect taxes, so account placement depends on the fund and your actual tax rates.</p>

    <Callout kind="do-this">
      <p>If a fall of about half would make you sell, hold fewer stocks: the
      <a href="/portfolios/cautious/">cautious portfolio</a> is one example with less stock exposure.
      Before adding protection, decide which loss it should address, how you will fund it and what ongoing
      cost you can accept. A fund's name does not answer those questions.</p>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Button href="/portfolios/cautious/">The cautious portfolio</Button>
    <Button href="/strategies/gold-and-bitcoin/" variant="outline">Gold and bitcoin</Button>
  </div>
</Base>
